{"title":"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A square has a perimeter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/9/81991bdaf7005f0b5a27da6a4478b8d949c430b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$28 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" />. The area of the square, in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/e/d2eda192a13e7edb51bd027dde93a73d51813d91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$cm^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"17\" />, is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/e/17e19416051e96d5b400fb3780488cc9782a45ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 196 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 784 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 64 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 49 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 56$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"452\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Perimeter\" title=\"Perimeter\">perimeter</a> of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Square\" title=\"Square\">square</a> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times its side length, so the side length of the square is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/9/5d959936c3ce18e09922799e193b9936105d2ea4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$28 \\text{ cm} \\div 4 = 7 \\text{ cm}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"138\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>The <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Area\" title=\"Area\">area</a> of a square is its side length multiplied by itself, so the area of the square (in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/e/cce95ec3eb5f2ff5452281e5b03c39757b163de2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" />) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/1/781a9c1af26d5cb26904c543824c6675a8d743d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 \\times 7 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(D) } 49}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"132\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 11\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" ali